<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sfax</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sfax is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-gabes">Gulf of Gabès</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and the country's second-largest city. It grew in the Aghlabid period, when its walls and Great Mosque were built in the 9th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> held it from 1148 until a revolt in 1156. It is a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> production, fishing and trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sfax.jpg" alt="Sfax" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: El Golli Mohamed · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ksar_Berromdhan002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sfax lies about 270 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> on a low coastal strip about 15 km wide, with an average altitude of 20 m. The rivers of the region are shallow and rarely reach the sea. The climate is hot and semi-arid, and because the city is sheltered from the winter storms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> it receives about half the rainfall of Tunis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The present city was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> in 849 on the site of the town of Taparura. By the end of the 10th century it was an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>. In the 12th century the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> described a thriving city with a stone wall, lively markets and a harbour, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> was of a quality found nowhere else. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> conquered it in 1148; it was liberated by a revolt in 1156 and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>. Venice mounted an unsuccessful invasion in 1185, as Sfax had become a base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> piracy.</p><p>The city returned to Hafsid rule around 1370. In 1534 the privateer Barberousse entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and reunited the towns of the coast, Sfax among them. Under the Husseinid dynasty the ramparts were restored, the Great Mosque was enlarged and in 1776 the Frankish quarter was built to the south for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians. Venetian ships bombarded the city four times in 1785–86.</p><p>After the Bardo Treaty of 1881 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> a French protectorate, an insurrection broke out in Sfax; French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ironclad">ironclads</a> bombarded it and troops took it on 16 July after hard fighting. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> used it as a major base in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> until British forces took it on 10 April 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sfax is the second most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, with 341,999 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2022. It is the country's largest fishing port and a centre of olive and nut processing, phosphate shipping and trade. Its medina, home to about 113,000 residents, is dominated by the Great Mosque and enclosed by walls 2,750 m long and seven to eleven metres high. The kasbah in its south-western corner, once an Aghlabid control tower and later army barracks, is now a museum of traditional architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sfax” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Mosque of Kairouan, the Great Mosque of Sousse, the Great Mosque of <b>Sfax</b>, the ribats of Sousse and Monastir and the reservoirs of Kairouan survive to the present, and the Kairouan mosque's dome, carved minbar and lustre-tiled mihrab remain landmarks of early Islamic art.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His admiral George of Antioch took Tripoli in 1146, and in 1148 Mahdia, Sousse and <b>Sfax</b> fell as the Zirid ruler al-Hasan fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-africa">Kingdom of Africa</a></li></ul></section>
